<p>A thousand beta builds later, John Siracusa has finally launched his new app, a dock-and-palette utility called <a href="http://hypercritical.co/2026/11/23/jetty-pro">Jetty Pro</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  I’d like to introduce Jetty Pro: an app that creates customizable Dock-like floating palettes around the edges of your Mac’s displays. These palettes can contain any combination of files, folders, volumes, apps, Shortcuts, spacers, dividers, and even the Trash. If you’ve ever been unsatisfied with some aspect of how the Dock works, or if you’ve just wanted to augment the Dock with some additional palettes, try Jetty Pro and make your Mac’s interface work the way <em>you</em> want it to.
</p></blockquote>
<p>I&#8217;ve been using this app for months and I love it. It has very specifically filled a space in my Mac&#8217;s interface that has been empty <a href="https://www.dragthing.com">since DragThing died in 2019</a>, namely a palette full of arbitrary stuff like SSH connections, folders, macros, and URLs that I want to get to with a single click.</p>
<p>For a guy who has a reputation for liking things <em>just so</em>, you might think Jetty Pro does things only one way. That&#8217;s not true. One of the things John wants <em>just so</em> is for his apps to have a lot of user-configurable options. You can make palettes of various looks, with various behaviors, and have their behavior remain consistent or vary depending on what display you&#8217;re using.</p>
<p>(And yes, it is a <em>thorough</em> Dock replacement, so I can finally have a second Dock appear on the right side of my secondary display whenever I have it open.)</p>
<p>In John&#8217;s <a href="https://hypercritical.co/jetty-pro/">unsurprisingly detailed FAQ page</a> he explains why the app <a href="https://hypercritical.co/jetty-pro/#subscription">is available only on a subscription basis</a>—mostly, it&#8217;s because it will require a lot of maintenance due to its use of private Apple APIs. Those private APIs are also why it&#8217;s not available in the Mac App Store, but only direct from John.</p>
<p>Jetty Pro has a 14-day free trial, and then costs $10 for the first year. Oh, and there&#8217;s no non-Pro version of Jetty in the Mac App Store. John worked on one for a while, but it didn&#8217;t make sense. All the goodness of Jetty Pro is happening with John&#8217;s circumvention of Apple&#8217;s rules. He&#8217;s a rebel! And his attempt to slowly rebuild every feature of DragThing continues.</p>
